Z||000000||Carrolles||02-04-2000||01:25 PM||||I'm posting this here because I'm afraid it won't get much attention on the Totally Off topic area.<P>This is a non-Fiero question but maybe someone can help.<P>On my daughter's 1990 Mozda Protege the Temp gauge goes to zero when the auto trans downshifts and the revs go up.  It almost acts like the inverse of a tach.  As rpms go up temp goes down.  As soon as the revs go back down the temp returns to normal.  Coolant level is ok and the car does not overheat.<P>TIA,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||02-04-2000||01:42 PM||||I have a friend with a '92, and his does the same thing. Makes for some really funny comments. He's never found out why it does that.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||02-04-2000||02:07 PM||||Carroll,<P>This is totally a SWAG...<P>I think most temp gauges work by applying a fixed voltage across a variable resistance (the sensor). If the voltage changes the reaction of the guage to the same resistance would change. Could it be that the Tach is somehow making the temp gauge voltage change???<P>David.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||02-05-2000||09:41 PM||||This car doesn't have a tach but I was thinking along those lines also.  Maybe there is a loose ground or something causing feedback through the temp gauge wiring.<P>Thanks,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
